      "fieldData": {
       "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Grammar Quiz Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a grammar quiz?\", \"a\": \"A grammar quiz is a short test of whether someone can spot and fix errors in written sentences, from punctuation to commonly confused words. Each question shows a sentence and asks which version is correct, and the score shows which rules the taker knows and which need work.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a grammar quiz?\", \"a\": \"Start with 5 to 8 rules your takers actually get wrong, write one sentence-based question per rule, and choose what each score band leads to. With Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder'>AI survey builder</a>, you describe the quiz in a prompt and get working questions plus a results table saved to a database you own.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between an English quiz and a grammar quiz?\", \"a\": \"An English quiz can cover anything in the subject: literature, vocabulary, spelling, comprehension, or grammar. A grammar quiz tests one slice, the rules for building correct sentences. If you are placing students or screening writers, the narrower quiz gives a cleaner signal, because every miss maps to a rule you can teach or flag.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I build a free grammar quiz for my website?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing. A free grammar quiz on a course site or class page is a sensible first build, and the same approach runs a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/geography'>geography quiz</a> when you need one.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many questions should a grammar quiz have?\", \"a\": \"Match the length to the job: 10 to 15 questions place students reliably, 8 to 10 screen applicants, and 5 work for a weekly class check. Write 1 or 2 grammar quiz questions per rule you care about and stop there. A taker should finish in one sitting.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should grammar quiz questions and answers appear together at the end?\", \"a\": \"Show them when the quiz teaches, and hide them when it screens. A class or placement quiz that reviews the grammar quiz questions and answers together at the end turns each miss into a lesson. A screening quiz keeps them hidden, since the score is the point.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can a grammar quiz place students into class levels?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Set score bands when you build it, with the lowest band feeding your foundations group, and each taker lands in a level the moment they finish. When the placement matters, pair it with a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/vocabulary'>vocabulary quiz</a>. One bad day on one test should not set a student's level alone.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I use a grammar quiz to screen freelance writers?\", \"a\": \"Yes, as a first gate. Set a pass mark, let the quiz send the decline or the next step on its own, and keep a short paid test piece for whoever clears it. The app emails each applicant their result, and editors see only the names that passed.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I see which rules students miss most on a grammar quiz?\", \"a\": \"Save every answer with the rule it tests, then sort or filter by rule. In an app built with Emergent, each response is a row in a table you own. One filter shows the rule a class keeps missing, by week or by student, with no copying into a spreadsheet.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I reuse my grammar quiz app for another subject?\", \"a\": \"Yes. The questions are content; the scoring and the results table are the app. A prompt swaps the question set, and the build that runs your grammar quiz can run a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/bible'>bible quiz</a> for a youth group next term.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
      }
     },
     {
      "id": "6ad227a29a2777aa13bd1f5c",
      "fieldData": {
       "asqb---how-to-step-7-des": "Six weeks later, resend only the statement the manager chose, worded exactly as before. Identical wording is what makes the two scores comparable, and the team sees that what it wrote in the first round led somewhere."
      }
     },
